hello — minimal aarch64 sysl kernel (QEMU virt)
===============================================

Rewrites the C hello milestone in sysl so the aarch64 port runs
through the same toolchain as x86_64 (sysl -> LLVM IR ->
clang -> aarch64 ELF). UART access is direct MMIO writes to the
PL011 at `0x09000000`; QEMU's firmware leaves the UART already
initialised, so we only poll the busy flag and stuff bytes.

    module oskit.arch.aarch64.board.virt

PL011 register addresses on the QEMU virt machine.

    val UART0_DR: i64 = 0x09000000
    val UART0_FR: i64 = 0x09000018
    val UARTFR_TXFF = 0x20

Write one byte to the UART data register, waiting for the TX FIFO
to drain first. `volatile` keeps the read of the flag register from
being hoisted out of the busy-wait loop.

    uart_putc(c: int)
        volatile var fr: *i32 = *i32(UART0_FR)
        var busy = 1
        while busy != 0
            busy = int(*fr) & UARTFR_TXFF
        var dr: *i32 = *i32(UART0_DR)
        *dr = i32(c)

Write a NUL-terminated sysl string, translating `'\n'` into CRLF
for terminal emulators that expect carriage returns.

    uart_puts(s: string)
        val p = *byte(s)
        val n = len(s)
        for var i = 0; i < n; i++
            val ch = int(p[i])
            if ch == 10
                uart_putc(13)
            uart_putc(ch)

Entry point invoked by `boot.s`. Currently just prints greetings;
it will grow into the real kernel initialisation as the aarch64
port matures.

    kernel_main()
        uart_puts("SLIX aarch64 boot (sysl)\n")
        uart_puts("Hello, aarch64!\n")
